body:
Catching up on the replies since my March 29 comment.

@newjordan — Neither OpenAI employee nor karma farming. I'm a competitor (PR #769, PROTEUS+STYX stack at 0.8495 BPB). I reviewed the backlog because 495+ March PRs had zero review as of April 9 and the maintainers were focused on the merge chain, not compliance. The tooling disclosure is in every comment I post. As for agents vs. researchers — agreed. The bottleneck is knowing what to try, not typing speed. I run about 40-60 ablations a day too. The tools are tools; the judgment about what matters is human.

@HKati — The three-layer separation (validity conditions / enforcement harnesses / evidence surface) is the right decomposition. The reviews I've been posting operate at layer 3 (evidence surface inspection) using simple layer 2 tools (AST classifier + CPU smoke test), grounded in layer 1 (the 4 conditions from this issue + the #402/#677 rulings). When those layers get conflated, reviews break — I've seen it in my own work when the smoke runner false-positived on sibling imports and I had to retract 34 reviews. A machine-readable evidence surface spec would be worth a standalone proposal if you're interested in writing it up.

@ClassicLarry — Agreed on the audience calibration. The verbose framing catches the cases where someone's agent generated code they don't fully understand, which is a lot of submissions in the March-April wave. Your masking test is a good diagnostic — it just shouldn't be the only one.

@asuramaya — Thanks for sharing heinrich. Haven't had a chance to evaluate it yet but the masking approach is a useful complement to the static-analysis path.

@anthony-maio — Appreciate the endorsement. The 4-condition framework from this issue is what I've been using as the baseline for 771 reviews across the repo. A CONTRIBUTING.md PR formalizing it would be a net positive.
